{"number": 62, "text": "                                                                                      Section C Pronunciation in conversation\n\n\n                      Exercises\n\n             28.1\n                     In the conversation below, the complete forms of the sections in bold are given. Listen and decide\n                     when  the speakers actually use ellipsis (write E) or near ellipsis (write NE).\n               B71\n\n\n                                                    AN                                           OS\n\n\n                       : What  are you making?     __..! NE\n                    > >\n                       : It’s a cake for Richard’s birthday.\n                       : It’s amazing, isn’t it?\n                        Do  you  think      like it?  ww.\n                        I'm sure he will, although he’s a bit fussy about food, isn’t he?\n                        Have  you  seen this?\n                       : Wow!  Is that a real flower?\n                       : No, it’s made from sugar\n                       : When  does it have to be ready?\n                       : It’s his birthday tomorrow. Do you  know  where  he is NOW?\n                             no idea.\n\n             28.2\n                     Listen to these conversations. Press ‘pause’ before each B part and read it aloud. (Use near ellipsis of\n                     the word(s) in bold.) Then press ‘play’ again and compare your pronunciation with what follows.\n               B72\n                      1  A: Have  you heard  from  Paul recently? B: I’ve just phoned him.\n                         A: My  shoes feel tight. B: Have you got the right ones?\n\n                         A: I retired last year. B: What are you doing now?\n                         A: We’re having  a barbeque  tonight. B: It’s a good job it’s not raining.\n                         A: Marco’s  got a new  job. B: What  does he do?\n                         A: Do  you like my new  hat?  B: Is that a hat?\n                      7  A: Pat looks really ill. B: She’s got a terrible cold.\n                      8  A: Whattime   is it? B: It’s half past.\n                      9  A: We  got that painting in Spain. B: Do you  remember   exactly where we  bought  it?\n                     10  A: Have  you taken  my  money?   B: What  are you talking about?\n                      11 A: Do  you think we  can cut across that field? B: I’m pretty sure we can.\n                     12  A: What’s  that thing? B: It’s called a dibber.\n                     13  A: I can’t find my gloves. B: Are these yours?\n                     14  A: We’re having  a brown-bag   lunch. B: What  does  that mean?\n                     15  A: We  should be in Milton  in about ten minutes.  B: Do you  know  where  to go when  we\n                            get there?\n\n                       Follow up: Ellipsis and near ellipsis happen in informal speech in most languages. Listen to conversations in\n                       your first language (or better still, listen to a recording) and find examples.\n\n\n                                                                          English Pronunciation in Use (Advanced)     63", "char_count": 3167, "mean_conf": 88.57, "word_count": 414, "words": [{"text": "28.1", "x0": 235, "y0": 357, "x1": 330, "y1": 395, "conf": 95, "bold": true}, {"text": "B71", "x0": 272, "y0": 423, "x1": 327, "y1": 460, "conf": 85, "bold": false}, {"text": "28.2", "x0": 235, "y0": 1891, "x1": 338, "y1": 1929, "conf": 95, "bold": true}, {"text": "B72", "x0": 276, "y0": 1958, "x1": 328, "y1": 1981, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "PES", "x0": 396, "y0": 1245, "x1": 434, "y1": 1822, "conf": 19, "bold": false}, {"text": "PSP", "x0": 378, "y0": 1511, "x1": 442, "y1": 1645, "conf": 17, "bold": false}, {"text": ">", "x0": 378, "y0": 1458, "x1": 442, "y1": 1490, "conf": 48, "bold": false}, {"text": "Y", "x0": 378, "y0": 1404, "x1": 442, "y1": 1432, 